WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.071 --> 00:00:06.954
It was almost dark as Christine continued her story for Raoul on the roof of the opera house. "When

2
00:00:06.955 --> 00:00:17.508
I woke up on my first morning in Erik's house," Christine began, "I was in a large, comfortable bedroom that

3
00:00:17.509 --> 00:00:24.645
included a bed, a couch, and a dining table. Erik soon appeared with his arms full of parcels. He

4
00:00:24.646 --> 00:00:32.638
had brought me food and clothing." "Everything you'd need for a long stay underground," Raoul said sarcastically. "What a good

5
00:00:32.639 --> 00:00:41.565
host." Christine ignored him and went on with her story. "I taunted Erik to take off his mask, but

6
00:00:41.566 --> 00:00:50.218
he said I'd never see his face. He predicted that, after a few days, I'd be so used to

7
00:00:50.218 --> 00:00:57.881
his mask that I'd no longer care about the face underneath." Raoul took Christine's hands in his hands. "Oh, my

8
00:00:58.770 --> 00:01:06.510
poor darling. How frightened you must have been!" Christine nodded in agreement. "When I ate breakfast a short while

9
00:01:06.510 --> 00:01:11.710
later, Erik sat with me, not eating. I tried to make polite conversation, but he refused to tell me

10
00:01:11.711 --> 00:01:23.637
anything about his past. When I finished, he led me into the next room, which was painted black, with one

11
00:01:23.738 --> 00:01:30.467
organ along one wall. On a desk was a book of music, covered in red notes." "Red? Music is

12
00:01:30.468 --> 00:01:37.869
usually written with black notes. What was in this book?" "Erik said it was Don Juan Triumphant , a grand

13
00:01:37.870 --> 00:01:46.841
work he'd been composing for 20 years. When I asked him to play some of it for me, he

14
00:01:46.842 --> 00:01:53.088
refused. We went to the flower-filled drawing room, where he played opera music on the piano. While I was

15
00:01:54.383 --> 00:02:02.422
singing with him, I was overcome with the urge to rip off his mask. And that's just what I did!

16
00:02:02.423 --> 00:02:09.442
Oh, the horror!" "Horror! Horror! Horror!" As Christine's words echoed on the opera house roof, Raoul and Christine clasped

17
00:02:09.443 --> 00:02:24.363
each other tightly. Raoul remembered the earlier echo they'd heard. "It's strange that this night, which seems so peaceful,

18
00:02:24.364 --> 00:02:36.837
should be so full of haunting sounds." Above them stars twinkled in the clear, dark sky. Christine shivered and went

19
00:02:36.838 --> 00:02:43.645
on with her story. "If I live to be a hundred, I'll never forget the cry of grief and

20
00:02:43.646 --> 00:02:52.468
rage that Erik uttered when I saw his face. Imagine a skull, Raoul, with black holes for the eyes, nose,

21
00:02:53.326 --> 00:02:58.846
and mouth. I later learned that you can only see his blazing eyes in the dark! Erik was furious

22
00:02:58.847 --> 00:03:06.280
and hissed at me. 'You weren't content just to hear me! You had to see my face! Are you

23
00:03:06.281 --> 00:03:12.543
happy now? When a woman has seen my face, she belongs to me forever!'" "Enough!" Raoul cried. "Tell me where

24
00:03:12.544 --> 00:03:17.862
the house on the lake is, and I will kill him! Once you escaped from this villain, why on

25
00:03:17.863 --> 00:03:25.895
earth did you ever go back to him?" "Be quiet, my love, and I'll tell you," Christine said. "At

26
00:03:25.896 --> 00:03:31.358
first Erik planned to let me go. He believed I'd come back—as long as I thought he was handsome. But

27
00:03:31.359 --> 00:03:40.044
once I saw his face, he knew I'd run away for good. 'So I shall keep you here!' he

28
00:03:40.045 --> 00:03:44.648
announced. He couldn't understand why I had wanted to see his face. His own father never wanted to see it,

29
00:03:44.649 --> 00:03:52.535
and his mother gave him his first mask, so she wouldn't have to look at him." Raoul again embraced

30
00:03:53.189 --> 00:03:58.042
Christine, who was on the verge of tears. "Erik rushed from the drawing room," Christine went on, "and I

31
00:03:58.043 --> 00:04:06.973
soon heard him playing the organ in the next room. Obviously he had turned to music, trying to forget the

32
00:04:06.974 --> 00:04:12.108
horror of the moment. I was sure he was playing Don Juan Triumphant because the music was different from

33
00:04:12.109 --> 00:04:20.061
anything I'd ever heard. It expressed every emotion, every suffering that humans are capable of—all in one awful, magnificent

34
00:04:20.062 --> 00:04:29.168
sob. The music drew me onward, and I opened the door. As I entered the room, Erik stood but dared

35
00:04:29.169 --> 00:04:43.239
not turn in my direction. I called out, 'Erik, show me your face!'" Raoul was amazed at her bravery.

36
00:04:43.240 --> 00:04:48.153
"And did he turn around?" "Wait!" Christine held up her hand to silence her fiancé. "I told Erik that if

37
00:04:48.153 --> 00:04:52.843
I ever shuddered when I looked at him, it was because I was thinking of his genius, not his

38
00:04:53.577 --> 00:05:02.946
face. Then he turned around because he believed me. He fell at my feet with words of love, never

39
00:05:02.947 --> 00:05:14.699
noticing I'd closed my eyes to avoid seeing him." "And then what happened?" Raoul asked. "I lied to him every

40
00:05:14.700 --> 00:05:26.385
day. My lies were as hideous as the person who inspired them, but they were the price of my

41
00:05:26.386 --> 00:05:32.770
freedom. I burned his mask and managed to convince him that I was not bothered by his appearance. Gradually

42
00:05:32.771 --> 00:05:40.938
I built up his trust. He walked with me on the banks of the lake and rowed me across its

43
00:05:40.938 --> 00:05:47.697
dark waters. Toward the end of my captivity, he led me through some gates to a street near the

44
00:05:48.185 --> 00:05:53.179
opera house. There a carriage awaited that took us for rides in the park at night." Raoul shook his head.

45
00:05:53.180 --> 00:06:00.396
"You endured so much, but how did you persuade him to let you go?" "After two weeks, during which

46
00:06:00.397 --> 00:06:09.986
I alternated between pity and horror, he believed me when I said I'd return to him." Raoul groaned. "And

47
00:06:09.987 --> 00:06:18.385
you actually went back, Christine! Why would you do such a thing after you'd escaped?" "When we said good-bye, he

48
00:06:18.386 --> 00:06:31.911
uttered terrible threats. However, it wasn't his threats that made me keep my word, but his sobs when we

49
00:06:31.912 --> 00:06:37.612
said good-bye. His sobs bound me to that unfortunate man. Poor Erik!" Raoul stood up. "You say you love

50
00:06:37.612 --> 00:06:47.201
me, but you'd only been free a few hours when you went back to him. Remember the masked ball? You

51
00:06:48.103 --> 00:06:53.559
told me it was over between us, and you returned to him through your dressing room mirror." "I know

52
00:06:53.559 --> 00:07:00.117
my behavior upset you, but you must understand why I returned to him. And that each visit increased my horror

53
00:07:00.118 --> 00:07:10.791
of him." Christine gave Raoul a pleading look. "I'm so frightened!" "You're afraid of Erik, but do you really

54
00:07:10.792 --> 00:07:18.687
love me? If Erik were good-looking, would you love me, Christine?" Christine stood and wrapped her arms around Raoul.

55
00:07:18.688 --> 00:07:27.979
"Oh, Raoul, if I didn't love you, would I let you kiss me?" As Raoul kissed Christine, there was a

56
00:07:27.980 --> 00:07:41.906
crack of thunder. The two young people hurried from the roof, avoiding the approaching storm—and the shadow stalking them.

